{"event":"trap-result","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-4","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-4/trap-0","ordinal":0,"result":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.17797","title":"Vision-as-a-Tool: Visual Harness for Multimodal Agentic Coding","authors":"Haolin Yang , Wenshan Wu , Jin Jiang , Yu Cheng , Yan Xia , Ting Song , Yangyu Huang , Zhiliang Peng , Wenhui Wang , Hangbo Bao , Yuzhe Zhang , Wen-Da Wei , Tengchao Lv , Li Dong , Lei Cui , Furu Wei","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Multimodal agentic coding requires robust visual reasoning to analyze observations and evaluate generated artifacts. Current frameworks largely rely on a Thinking-with-Images paradigm, where agents depend on their intrinsic vision to execute visually grounded tasks. However, this architectural coupling introduces a fundamental bottleneck. Agent performance remains strictly bounded by internal perceptual limitations. Furthermore, text-only agents lack native perception. Without specialized tools, they cannot perform visual reasoning. To overcome these constraints, we introduce the Vision-as-a-Tool (VaaT) paradigm. Prior visual tool-use merely manipulates images for an agent's intrinsic vision to read. In contrast, VaaT reframes general-purpose multimodal large language models (MLLMs) as callable external utilities. As a result, text-only agents can completely offload visual reasoning during agentic execution. Meanwhile, vision-capable agents can dynamically augment their native perception on demand. We instantiate this paradigm in VHarness, a unified framework driven by five atomic actions. We comprehensively evaluate VHarness across Image-to-HTML, Image-to-SVG, and Image-to-3D generation tasks. Experimental results demonstrate that VHarness consistently improves visual fidelity and geometric accuracy across both text-only and multimodal coding agents. These findings highlight that modularizing visual reasoning into callable utilities provides a flexible and scalable foundation for future agentic coding systems.","numbers":[],"method":["To overcome these constraints, we introduce the Vision-as-a-Tool (VaaT) paradigm."],"caveats":["However, this architectural coupling introduces a fundamental bottleneck.","Agent performance remains strictly bounded by internal perceptual limitations."],"topics":["vision-multimodal"],"score":6,"fullText":true,"readChars":6755,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17797","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.17797"}}
{"event":"branch-done","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-4","parentForkId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0","branch":4,"outcome":{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.17797","title":"Vision-as-a-Tool: Visual Harness for Multimodal Agentic Coding","authors":"Haolin Yang , Wenshan Wu , Jin Jiang , Yu Cheng , Yan Xia , Ting Song , Yangyu Huang , Zhiliang Peng , Wenhui Wang , Hangbo Bao , Yuzhe Zhang , Wen-Da Wei , Tengchao Lv , Li Dong , Lei Cui , Furu Wei","submitted":"13 Nov 2026","abstract":"Multimodal agentic coding requires robust visual reasoning to analyze observations and evaluate generated artifacts. Current frameworks largely rely on a Thinking-with-Images paradigm, where agents depend on their intrinsic vision to execute visually grounded tasks. However, this architectural coupling introduces a fundamental bottleneck. Agent performance remains strictly bounded by internal perceptual limitations. Furthermore, text-only agents lack native perception. Without specialized tools, they cannot perform visual reasoning. To overcome these constraints, we introduce the Vision-as-a-Tool (VaaT) paradigm. Prior visual tool-use merely manipulates images for an agent's intrinsic vision to read. In contrast, VaaT reframes general-purpose multimodal large language models (MLLMs) as callable external utilities. As a result, text-only agents can completely offload visual reasoning during agentic execution. Meanwhile, vision-capable agents can dynamically augment their native perception on demand. We instantiate this paradigm in VHarness, a unified framework driven by five atomic actions. We comprehensively evaluate VHarness across Image-to-HTML, Image-to-SVG, and Image-to-3D generation tasks. Experimental results demonstrate that VHarness consistently improves visual fidelity and geometric accuracy across both text-only and multimodal coding agents. These findings highlight that modularizing visual reasoning into callable utilities provides a flexible and scalable foundation for future agentic coding systems.","numbers":[],"method":["To overcome these constraints, we introduce the Vision-as-a-Tool (VaaT) paradigm."],"caveats":["However, this architectural coupling introduces a fundamental bottleneck.","Agent performance remains strictly bounded by internal perceptual limitations."],"topics":["vision-multimodal"],"score":6,"fullText":true,"readChars":6755,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17797","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.17797"}}}
